WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.984
The unit is a completed task.

2
00:00:02.264 --> 00:00:04.632
Week 1 put your service into production.

3
00:00:04.912 --> 00:00:08.603
This week is about what it costs to run and how long people wait.

4
00:00:08.883 --> 00:00:13.256
Start with the unit of measure, because the wrong unit leads to the wrong decision.

5
00:00:13.536 --> 00:00:19.445
Per-token prices are what the pricing page shows, and per-request cost is what a log line shows.

6
00:00:19.725 --> 00:00:21.837
Neither is what the business pays for.

7
00:00:22.117 --> 00:00:30.288
The business pays for completed tasks: a ticket resolved, a document summarized correctly, a pull request that passes review.

8
00:00:30.568 --> 00:00:37.779
Anthropic’s cost guidance makes this its organizing idea: optimize cost per completed task, not per token.

9
00:00:38.059 --> 00:00:50.240
A model with a higher sticker price can be the cheaper choice if it finishes in fewer turns, and a cheaper model that fails still bills its tokens, then the retry, then whatever the failure costs downstream.

10
00:00:50.520 --> 00:01:04.173
The definition is simple and worth writing down for your team: cost per completed task = total spend on all attempts, including retries and failed tasks, divided by the number of tasks that met the quality bar.

11
00:01:04.453 --> 00:01:05.947
Two consequences follow.

12
00:01:06.227 --> 00:01:09.064
First, you can’t compute it without an outcome check.

13
00:01:09.344 --> 00:01:17.728
A log of tokens tells you spend; only your eval or a production signal (resolved, accepted, not reopened) tells you completion.

14
00:01:18.008 --> 00:01:23.000
Second, cost and quality become one number you can compare across configurations.

15
00:01:23.280 --> 00:01:29.168
Anthropic’s published runs show why that matters: on one coding benchmark, Claude Sonnet 5.

16
00:01:29.448 --> 00:01:31.901
5 at high effort and Claude Opus 5.

17
00:01:32.181 --> 00:01:44.491
5 at low effort solved about the same share of tasks, with the Opus configuration costing about 7 percent more per solved task, even though Opus’s per-token prices are twice Sonnet’s.

18
00:01:44.771 --> 00:01:48.312
Per-token prices alone would have predicted a much bigger gap.
